  {
    "event_key": "whr",
    "display_name": "Waist-to-Hip Ratio",
    "input_unit": "ratio (waist ÷ hip, decimal)",
    "gender_split": true,
    "age_split": false,
    "bands": {
      "male": [
        {"threshold": "a", "points": 0},
        {"threshold": 0, "points": 10},
        {"threshold": 0.85, "points": 10},
        {"threshold": 0.86, "points": 7.5},
        {"threshold": 0.91, "points": 5},
        {"threshold": 0.96, "points": 2.5},
        {"threshold": 1.005, "points": 0},
        {"threshold": 100, "points": 0}
      ],
      "female": [
        {"threshold": "a", "points": 0},
        {"threshold": 0, "points": 10},
        {"threshold": 0.75, "points": 10},
        {"threshold": 0.76, "points": 7.5},
        {"threshold": 0.81, "points": 5},
        {"threshold": 0.86, "points": 2.5},
        {"threshold": 0.905, "points": 0},
        {"threshold": 100, "points": 0}
      ]
    },
    "notes": "Lower ratio = better score (VLOOKUP ascending). Display table labels the male cutoff as '> 1.00' for 0 pts and '0.96–1.00' for 2.5 pts, but the lookup threshold is 1.005 — meaning a ratio of exactly 1.00 scores 2.5 pts (not 0). Similarly, female display says '> 0.90' for 0 pts but lookup uses 0.905, so exactly 0.90 scores 2.5 pts. The offsets appear intentional: they make the displayed ranges (0.96–1.00 = 2.5 pts) mathematically precise. The sentinel 'a' represents non-numeric or blank input."
  },
